{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>לֹא הִבִּיט אָוֶן בְּיַעֲקֹב</b> — He has not beheld iniquity in Jacob. This may be explained according to the words of the masters of blessed memory (Ra'aya Mehayimna, vol. 3, para. 2) who said that through a person's sin, an impression of the power of evil is engraved in the limb with which he committed it, and this is the aspect of the blemish (pegam) mentioned in their words of blessed memory. And the meaning of ״אָוֶן״ is the power of evil drawn from the transgression, as it says (Proverbs 30:20) ״לֹא פָעַלְתִּי אָוֶן״, and it is written (Psalms 125:5) ״יוֹלִיכֵם ה׳ אֶת פּוֹעֲלֵי הָאָוֶן״. Therefore it says <b>לֹא הִבִּיט אָוֶן</b> — the impression of the evil effect is not even in Jacob, that is, the multitudes of the people who are called by the name Jacob, for all of them guard against evil deeds. And even if they err from the way of the intellect, the ״אָוֶן״ — which is the evil portion — is not fixed in their souls, as it says (Song of Songs 4:7) ״כֻּלָּךְ יָפָה וּמוּם אֵין בָּךְ״, meaning that sin does not produce in him a permanent blemish but only a superficial passing-over that is washed away through rinsing and scrubbing, as hinted by the masters of blessed memory (Song of Songs Rabbah) in the verse ״שְׁחוֹרָה אֲנִי וְנָאוָה״ — see there. And His saying <b>וְלֹא רָאָה עָמָל בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל</b>, meaning: even a blemish that requires labor (amal) to remove it is not among those called Israel, who are the righteous." "<b>Or</b> it may intend His saying <b>לֹא הִבִּיט</b> — ״אָוֶן״ is the sin of thought, which is also called אָוֶן, as it says (Psalms 66:18) ״אָוֶן אִם רָאִיתִי בְלִבִּי״ — that HaShem does not look at the evil thought. <b>בְּיַעֲקֹב</b>, meaning: the evil thought does not produce in Jacob the impression of ״אָוֶן״, which is the evil branch. Therefore it specified the language of הַבָּטָה (looking intently), which is more than רְאִיָּה (seeing), meaning: for although at first sight He sees an evil thought, when He looks into its inner effect there is no אָוֶן. And it specified <b>אָוֶן בְּיַעֲקֹב</b>, meaning: that sin does not produce its impression in him, and this applies even to the multitudes of the people who are designated by the name Jacob. Corresponding to the righteous among them, it says <b>וְלֹא רָאָה</b>, meaning: even an improper thought called עָמָל, with which the evil impulse labors through the power of thought to turn it from the way of the intellect — and the righteous, their heart is hollow within them (i.e., immune), and that is His saying <b>וְלֹא רָאָה עָמָל בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל</b>. It further intends to say that the righteous, even though they perform commandments and all their occupation is in Torah, do not feel that they have any עָמָל, as it says (Psalms 73:16) ״עָמָל הוּא בְעֵינָי״ — but on the contrary, like a person who derives fragrance and like a person who takes delight in pleasures, because of the abundance of their desire for Torah. It also intends, in the manner of the verse (Proverbs 8:14) ״אֲנִי בִינָה לִי גְבוּרָה״, that when the righteous engage in Torah and persist in it, they have no עָמָל in the labor of Torah, for on the contrary it gives them גְּבוּרָה (strength)." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend to say that on account of the merit of the two levels that exist in Israel, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not exact punishment from the wicked of the world among the nations, and in this [matter] He does not destroy them; nor does He grow weary, as it were, in maintaining the world and bearing the burden of all things the world requires. And that is His saying <b>לֹא הִבִּיט אָוֶן</b> that exists in the world — for the sake of Jacob; and He does not see the עָמָל that He labored, as it were, in bearing the world and in its provisions and in supplying its needs — all of this is of no concern to Him for the sake of Israel, and that is His saying <b>בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל</b> — for the sake of Israel (literally: in Israel). And so you will find that when Israel walk in the way of Torah and commandments, HaShem gives His goodness to the world, and the nations too are sustained and benefit together. Henceforth, those who seek to uproot this nation seek to destroy the world and its inhabitants. It further intends in His saying ״וְלֹא רָאָה עָמָל בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל״ that the righteous — every whisper of theirs — produce gratification of spirit before HaShem, and they do not weary Him, as it were, as it says (Malachi 2:17) ״הוֹגַעְתֶּם ה׳ בְּדִבְרֵיכֶם״; therefore it says ״וְלֹא רָאָה עָמָל בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל״." "<b>ה׳ אֱלֹהָיו עִמּוֹ וּתְרוּעַת מֶלֶךְ בּוֹ</b> — YHWH his Elohim is with him, and the King's acclamation is in him. This corresponds to the two levels He mentioned: those called by the name Jacob, who are the multitudes of the people, and those designated by the name Israel, who are the righteous. <b>ה׳ אֱלֹהָיו עִמּוֹ</b> — these are the righteous who act out of love; therefore it says ״YHWH,״ which is the name of חֶסֶד (lovingkindness), to say that the righteous person's service is not from the side of judgment but rather he loves the good for its being good. And it specified ״אֱלֹהָיו״ so that you should not think that if YHWH chastises him he would turn back; therefore it says ״אֱלֹהָיו״ — that even though He chastises him, he stands firm in the keeping of his love. And corresponding to the class of the multitudes called by the name Jacob, it says: <b>וּתְרוּעַת מֶלֶךְ בּוֹ</b> — for he serves out of fear, from the side of judgment." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend to say a praise of their attainment — that HaShem did not choose to cause His name to dwell except with them, and further that He implanted in them a supernal power such that they are able to destroy whatever they set their eyes upon, like a king of the world, and that is His saying <b>וּתְרוּעַת מֶלֶךְ בּוֹ</b>, meaning: there is no need for HaShem to destroy before them; rather, in them themselves this level exists. And so you will find that the righteous — wherever they set their eyes to destroy among all created beings — they have the capacity." "<b>Further</b>, it may be interpreted according to the saying of the Sages, of blessed memory (Berakhot 61b), concerning Rabbi Akiva — that they were raking his flesh with iron combs, and he prolonged the recitation of אחד, etc. — and this is the meaning of the verse <b>HaShem his Elohim is with him</b>: it means that he binds his soul to HaShem his Elohim with the utmost love, even though the king's onslaught is upon him, with a rod of iron with which they rake his flesh — and even at the very moment that the rod is upon him, while they were raking, etc. — nonetheless, HaShem his Elohim is with him."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {5 "The verse 'ופרושו הוא אמרו' is read as connecting the verse to the Rabbinic story of Rabbi Akiva's martyrdom; translated the flow of the argument accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:51.060761884Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}